<!--
  Declared breaks against 1.0.0-beta.1.1, permitted by the repository's prerelease-stability policy and
  required by it to be declared. Six changes account for every entry here:

  1. The Affidavit as the rules define it — the record gains its two companion confidence numbers, a
     field gains a binding and a mandatory marker, and the projection port gains the entity id an
     update-shaped record needs. The positional constructors and Deconstruct members of the affected
     records change shape as a result; construction through Affidavit.Create / ProvenanceTag's
     factories is unaffected.
  2. An approval policy returns a verdict rather than a bare requirement, so it can name this write's
     review window (the deadline is stamped from the policy result, after the chain) and so the
     framework can record why a Standing Order was held back. IApprovalPolicy and
     IApprovalPolicyEvaluator change their return types; the new members on IApprovalPolicy are
     default interface members, so an existing implementation compiles unchanged apart from the
     return type.
  3. The Docket row and the store contract as the rules define them. DocketEntry gains the execution
     outcome, the decision record, the attestation, the blocked marker, the accepted amendment state,
     the preserved late amendments, the lineage and the channel, so its positional constructor and
     Deconstruct change shape; IDocketStore gains the scoped, guarded, paged surface and drops the two
     unscoped expiry members the sweep used to call.
  4. A decision names who made it. IApprovalPolicy.EvaluateAsync regains the conversation identity the
     specification declares a policy is given, and the decision surface takes a principal.
  5. The wire as the rulebook defines it: the Evidence Card envelope carries the record's two
     companion numbers, whether a person must confirm, the blocked marker, the presentation hints, the
     warnings and the host's own verb, so its positional constructor and Deconstruct change shape.
     Build cards with EvidenceCardRequest.For, which fills every repeated number from the record it is
     given.
  6. A decision reaches a blocking caller as a gate-minted DecisionHandOff rather than a client-shaped
     response. IStreamingTransport.AwaitEvidenceCardResponseAsync and TryDeliverResponse change their
     types accordingly, and IDocketStore.MarkBlockedAsync takes the scope every other member that
     moves a row takes. UpdateAmendmentsAsync is removed: it was an unscoped, unguarded overwrite with
     no caller left, and a recorded fact is not edited in place.
  7. A write proposal carries the arguments the model passed, because an entry id is derived from
     the operation AND its arguments (GT-4): the record's positional constructor and Deconstruct
     gain one defaulted parameter, so a call site that already compiled still does.
  8. The store guard is total. UpdateReviewStatusAsync is removed for the same reason as
     UpdateAmendmentsAsync and a stronger one: it took an entry id and a status and nothing else, so
     anything holding the store could write any status onto any row in any tenant, without the
     expected-status compare-and-set, the tenant scope or the attestation the decision path checks.
     TransitionAsync is the replacement and checks all three. IDocketStore also gains
     CountPendingAsync, a count for the docket-depth gauge, which previously asked for every pending
     row in the deployment on a timer; a store implementing the interface implements one more member.

  See CHANGELOG.md's upgrade notes.
-->
